                        "1. Route the ticket to **atlas** if it involves billing or invoicing matters, such as incorrect charges, duplicate payments, billing plan changes, invoice corrections, or requests for receipts and billing records.\n\n2. Route the ticket to **harbor** if it describes an active technical outage or system malfunction, such as API errors, timeouts, webhook delivery failures, slow performance degradation, or broken functionality following a deployment.\n\n3. Route the ticket to **beacon** if it concerns authentication or account security, including login failures, password reset issues, multi-factor authentication problems, locked accounts, unauthorized access, or suspicious account activity.\n\n4. Route the ticket to **quill** if it is a feature request, product improvement suggestion, how-to question, or general product usage inquiry that does not involve an active outage, billing issue, or security concern.\n\n5. Distinguish **harbor** (active system failure affecting functionality) from **quill** (general usage questions about how a feature works); if the user reports something is broken or degraded right now, prefer harbor.\n\n6. Distinguish **beacon** (security and authentication) from **harbor** (general technical outages); if the core issue is about identity verification, account access control, or suspicious credential activity, prefer beacon even if the symptom appears technical.\n\n7. Distinguish **atlas** (billing operations) from **harbor** (system failures); if automated billing emails or receipts are failing due to a deploy or system error rather than a billing data issue, prefer harbor.\n\n8. When a ticket combines elements of multiple teams (e.g., a billing question accompanied by a feature request), route based on the primary complaint or the most urgent issue described in the ticket."
